This attempt tries a different structure: square z directly, then apply c2 and c1 as plaintext multiplications. It uses one more plaintext multiplication than the best, so it will probably not win. I haven't found a cheaper algebraic form, because every constant must go through a ciphertext multiplication.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext s = square(z);
  Ciphertext t = s * c2;
  Ciphertext t2 = t + t;
  Ciphertext h = z * c1;
  Ciphertext r = t2 + h;
  r = r - c2;
  r = r + c0;
  r.set_output("y");
}
```

I haven't found a circuit cheaper than attempt 2. Its form, (c1 + 2·c2·z)·z + c0 − c2 with z = x·alpha, is the best I've found: 2 plaintext multiplications, 1 ciphertext multiplication, 3 rescales and 1 mod switch. This attempt resubmits it unchanged.

